---
/**
 * SKETCHES UNDER THE WORDS, 26 Nov 2026, evening (the same founder: "they need to be underneath the
 * text, and then ... the sketches side by side"): every sketch section uses AreaSide below, two to a
 * row. On Belsize Park, the wall-to-wall wardrobe, the hall coat cupboard, the cornice and the stair
 * sketches were redrawn after the founder found them weak.
 *
 * SKETCHES, 26 Nov 2026, later (a founder: "verify please all the copy and their sketch and see if
 * the copy aligns with the sketch"): the house-front drawings matched the old copy, not these words, so
 * every section now has sketches of the joinery its words describe (src/data/area-drawings.ts, the
 * 26 Nov block; AreaSide takes a list where the words name two pieces): top cupboards and sliding doors; each alcove measured; narrow kitchen plan and appliance run; the bay seat (kept); skirting kept behind a cupboard; the van and the protected way in.
 *
 * REWRITTEN TO SS_WRITING_FOR_KASPAR.md, 26 Nov 2026 (a founder: rewrite the
 * remaining area pages to the writing style he comments on; ops rulings D3 and
 * D7). Every sentence above the prices is new, in "we" and "you", about rooms
 * these houses have: no council or history narration, no source, date, listing
 * grade or house number in a sentence, no "carpentry", no definitions of house
 * words. Opening: one paragraph, two sentences. Four room sections (small
 * bedrooms, Leighton Road's alcoves, narrow kitchens, bay windows), then listed
 * homes and access on the guide's templates (K18, K19). PRICES, the price
 * section and the FAQ questions unchanged; the FAQ answers rewritten. One Ask
 * bracket, kentish-town-bay-seat: he has not confirmed window seats in bays (the
 * review link's card asks him). Drawings unchanged: each still fits its words.
 * FACT LIST (claim: source).
 *   Opening: the three products (service pages); Leighton Road and Kelly Street
 *     as street names (facts file K1, K2); a wardrobe up to the ceiling (his facts
 *     table, Islington 26 Nov); a kitchen planned around the route through the
 *     room (bespoke kitchens page, planning section, approved).
 *   Small bedrooms: narrow houses on Kelly Street and Castlehaven Road (facts
 *     file K2 and Group 2, "houses are narrow on narrow plots"); bedrooms "can
 *     be" small (the facts file's own inference, so "can"); a wardrobe across
 *     the full width of the wall (facts table, "wall to wall", Camden 23 Nov); separate top cupboards for bedding and
 *     luggage, sliding doors where swing space is limited (built-in wardrobe
 *     guide, approved 24 Oct).
 *   Leighton Road: late Georgian brick houses, stucco villas and Victorian
 *     terraces in one road (facts file K1); next-door houses differ (plot-by-plot
 *     building, K1, as the 19 Nov page put it); each alcove measured on its own,
 *     the sides can differ (facts table, Islington 26 Nov); wardrobes in alcoves
 *     (facts table, Camden 23 Nov); shelves over low cupboards (alcove price
 *     card, "base cupboards with shelving over").
 *   Kitchens: terraced houses around Lady Margaret Road and Dunolie Road narrow
 *     (facts file K1, "on narrow plots"); cabinets planned around doorways,
 *     windows and the route through the room, appliances confirmed before the
 *     final design so the cabinets line up (bespoke kitchens page, planning
 *     section). No kitchen job is claimed in the area.
 *   Bay windows: ground-floor bays on Falkland Road and Ascham Street (facts file
 *     K1 and Group 2, "common in the northern streets", so "many"); the seat is
 *     the Ask bracket; alcove units in the seat's finish (facts table, one finish
 *     across front and back room units, Camden 23 Nov; his portfolio's window
 *     seat beside alcove shelving, work-gallery.ts alcove-units-10).
 *   Listed homes: some homes on Leighton Road, Leverton Street and Kelly Street
 *     listed (facts file K1, K2); Camden's listed buildings page (K8); ask before
 *     work starts, skirting and panelling (facts table, Islington 26 Nov); the
 *     original features noted at the survey (his Camden text, 23 Nov). No cornice
 *     line: the facts file confirms none here, and three other area pages carry it.
 *   Access: unloading and parking agreed before fitting day (facts table); Kelly
 *     Street a narrow cul-de-sac (K2); Torriano Cottages on an unmade private
 *     lane (K1); the zones page's postcode search (K10); agreeing who arranges a
 *     permit (facts table, Islington 26 Nov), Camden's trade and other parking
 *     permits page (K12); floors and stairs protected (facts table).
 *   FAQ answers: the route around the bed and the view from the doorway
 *     (built-in wardrobe guide); recessed finger grips, push-to-open, agreeing
 *     how they open before the design is finished (wardrobe and alcove guides);
 *     a kitchen "made to measure, designed around the space" (K10, his result
 *     words), storage planned around what is used and where (bespoke kitchens
 *     page, planning section), Shaker or handleless doors (K11; both are his
 *     kitchens). The small-bedroom answer no longer carries sliding doors: the
 *     section above does.
 * Links checked 26 Nov 2026: the site pages answer 200 on the live site; the
 * three Camden pages answer plain curl with a Cloudflare challenge (403) and 200
 * with a browser's headers, their titles confirmed.
 * The notes below describe the 17 and 19 Nov text, which this replaces.
 *
 * Kentish Town, 17 November 2026. A draft for Kaspar to read on the review
 * link (F2F ops clients/structure-and-style/areas-review); held noindex by
 * areaKentishTownCheck until he has read it and his changes are in. Nothing
 * goes live before he has approved every area page (Denis, 15 Nov 2026).
 *
 * ON THE PRIMROSE HILL MODEL from the start (a founder's go, 17 Nov; Denis,
 * 15 Nov: "use the same design and structure for the next articles"; ops
 * README, "The structure rule, applied"; ops SS_AREA_PAGE_RULES_v1.0.md, a
 * draft, read for its plan items): address /areas/kentish-town/ with 301s from
 * /carpenter-kentish-town/; the title, heading, kicker and description lead
 * with fitted wardrobes, then bespoke kitchens, the register's photographs
 * (the reasons are in areas.ts); the opening carries "bespoke carpentry" and
 * "bespoke joinery" once each; a line drawing beside each section that
 * describes something (src/data/area-drawings.ts: three houses of different
 * dates side by side, a terrace of five behind iron railings, a long kitchen
 * in plan, a window seat in a canted bay), none where a section is rules or
 * access only; the guide prices as cards; register photos and one labelled AI
 * image (ssp-019, cropped to 3:2, AI white handleless wardrobes, Figma node
 * 34:2, and since 21 Nov 2026 an AI painted Shaker kitchen in place of ssp-020,
 * which showed the same kitchen as Islington's photo; since 22 Nov 2026
 * ssp-152, a sage Shaker kitchen from another job, in place of ssp-019, for
 * the same reason; areas.ts says why).
 *
 * Sources. Every local fact from the ops facts file
 * research/area-pages-2026-11-14/facts-kentish-town-2026-11-14.md: Camden's
 * Kentish Town Conservation Area Appraisal and Management Strategy (2011,
 * "K1"), its Kelly Street appraisal (March 2011, "K2"), Camden's listed
 * buildings, controlled parking zone, visitor permit and trade permit pages,
 * and the Planning Portal's internal walls page, all read 14 Nov 2026.
 *   Leighton Road: two and three storey early 19th century terraces, mostly
 *     stucco with some brick; built plot by plot after Joshua Prole Torriano
 *     sold small freeholds; late Georgian stock brick, 1830s stucco villas and
 *     mid-Victorian terraces in one street; brown or buff stock brick, or
 *     stucco in various colours; timber sashes with small panes up to the mid
 *     19th century (Leighton Road) and larger panes later (Leverton Street)
 *     (K1). Torriano Cottages: mid-Victorian two storey cottages (K1).
 *   Kelly Street: a narrow cul-de-sac of small two storey houses, brightly
 *     coloured stucco fronts, black cast-iron railings; Castlehaven Road's
 *     three storey terraces, two windows wide, with small rear courtyard
 *     gardens; "a rare intact example" of Victorian terraces west of Kentish
 *     Town Road, many lost to post-war rebuilding (K2).
 *   The northern streets (Falkland Road, Ascham Street, Lady Margaret Road):
 *     mostly three storey brick and stucco terraces on narrow plots, longer
 *     rear gardens, canted ground-floor bays, finished by 1894 (K1).
 *   Listed: Leighton Road Nos 20 to 28 and 64 to 70 at Grade II; Leverton
 *     Street Nos 2 to 26 even and 5 to 23 odd, stuccoed terraces of about
 *     1845 (K1); every building on Kelly Street and its railings (K2).
 *   Torriano Cottages on a winding unmade private lane (K1).
 *   Camden: every part of a listed building protected, works without consent
 *     a criminal offence even if the owner did not know (K8); Planning Portal:
 *     interior work generally needs no planning permission, a listed building
 *     can need listed building consent (K13); parking zone map, visitor
 *     permits bought by residents (all-day permits not offered in every
 *     zone), trade permits with no guaranteed space (K10 to K12). Camden's page did not confirm which Kentish Town streets
 *     are controlled, so the page sends readers to the map.
 * Left out: the Bartholomew Estate (its only description is an applicant's
 * heritage report, not Camden's own document), Inkerman and West Kentish Town
 * (not read), and any Article 4 Direction (none confirmed in force here).
 *
 * BROUGHT TO THE UPDATED COPY RULES, 19 Nov 2026 (a founder, in chat: "let's do
 * the 4th area page"). Written on 17 Nov, the day the area page rules v1.0 were
 * drafted. Four independent checks (copy rules with the playbook, facts, the
 * swap test against all nine other pages, and the AI picture under the
 * founders' 19 Nov picture rules) found, and this change fixes:
 *   - the opening: products first, in a frame no other page uses; "narrow"
 *     only for the terraces the sources call narrow (also in the description);
 *     the second line is Leighton Road's own mix (K1), not a line any area
 *     could carry;
 *   - the access promise as on the other pages ("Parking and deliveries", no
 *     "access"); no "we settle the layout first" (no source); the zones page
 *     "has a postcode search" (K10); a trade permit is "another option";
 *   - facts as the sources give them: canted bays "many", not all; the
 *     listed-consent rule from the Planning Portal (K13) for significant work,
 *     not for fitted furniture; plot-by-plot building, not "built separately";
 *     the Leverton Street terraces by number; no assumed three-sided bay or
 *     chimney breast;
 *   - trivia out (the landowner's name, "finished by 1894", the heritage aside
 *     on Kelly Street); "stock brick" and Grade II plain; sources named in
 *     the sentence; Castlehaven Road's width said once;
 *   - headings of the page's own (Kelly Street, kitchens, prices); a recheck
 *     then split a 28-word listed-buildings sentence and kept the parking
 *     search and the kitchens heading to what the sources say;
 *   - the AI plate's alt text: what this picture shows, not the template the
 *     other wardrobe alts share. The picture itself passed every rule.
 *   Left as they were: the guarantee and price lines, the kitchen FAQ answer
 *   (to check with Kaspar against the kitchens page), "Parking and deliveries
 *   are agreed" (as on the other pages; not in Kaspar's conduct list).
 *
 * WHOSE WORDS. From what he approved: the wardrobe guide's line on top
 * cupboards for bedding and luggage; the bespoke kitchens page's planning
 * section ("Doorways, windows and routes through the room, together with the
 * positions of sinks and appliances, affect where cabinet runs, tall units and
 * islands can sit", word for word); the guarantee; the fitted wardrobe,
 * kitchen and alcove price lines as approved on Hampstead. Window seats: his
 * own portfolio shows one (work-gallery.ts, alcove-units-10). One conduct
 * line: parking and deliveries agreed before installation, as on the other
 * area pages; it is not in Kaspar's conduct list, so it waits for his word. Everything else is new copy for him to check.
 *
 * CHECKED 17 Nov by three independent reviewers (copy rules, facts against
 * the facts file and approved text, the swap test against Camden, Primrose
 * Hill, Islington, Hampstead and Belsize Park). No must-fix. Changed on their
 * findings: the opening on the Primrose Hill pattern, its second sentence
 * from the cottages and terraces; his 6 Sep sentence on where much of the
 * wardrobe work is (Camden and Hampstead carry it) and the house hours
 * (Hampstead's) left out; the rooms-differ line (our inference) replaced by
 * the appraisal's window panes; "small houses" now "narrow" (Castlehaven Road
 * is three storeys); the kitchen section rebuilt on Camden's plots and
 * courtyards, without the intro line Hampstead's question already quotes or
 * the "long" kitchen no source gives; "cut to the angle" (a making method)
 * and "cut into the front" gone; the listed section's Planning Portal line
 * (Belsize Park's) cut and the street names checked as Grade II; the access
 * section's guess about section sizes cut, and the visitor permit line says
 * all-day permits are not offered in every zone; stucco, stock brick and
 * canted bays explained once; two drawing labels and two alts corrected.
 *
 * Street names are Camden's appraisals', describing the houses. None is a
 * place we claim to have worked. No review block: the one review that names
 * Kentish Town is left out on purpose (our reply of 13 Nov left the area out),
 * and no quotable review names the area.
 *
 * FAQ sources, all three new, none on another area page: "Where should a
 * wardrobe go in a small bedroom?" (Google People also ask on fitted wardrobes
 * small bedroom: "Where to put a closet in a small bedroom?", "closet" made
 * British), "How do handleless drawers work?" (People also ask, handleless
 * kitchen) and "What is a bespoke kitchen?" (People also ask, bespoke
 * kitchen); UK, English, desktop, the built-in browser, 17 Nov 2026. The box
 * for fitted wardrobes kentish town held costs (the price cards answer them),
 * a B&Q question (no other firms) and two depth questions (Camden already asks
 * "Is 500mm deep enough for a wardrobe?"); bespoke kitchens kentish town had
 * no box. Answers from what he approved: the wardrobe guide (the route around
 * the bed and the view from the doorway; sliding doors where swing space is
 * limited), the alcove units and wardrobe guides on handleless fronts
 * (recessed finger pulls, push-to-open, agree how they open before the final
 * detailing), and the bespoke kitchens page (its intro's first sentence and
 * its line on confirming appliances before the final design).
 */
import AreaPage from "../../components/AreaPage.astro";
import Wrap from "../../components/ds/Wrap.astro";
import SectionHead from "../../components/ds/SectionHead.astro";
import Faq from "../../components/ds/Faq.astro";
import Link from "../../components/ds/Link.astro";
import AreaSide from "../../components/AreaSide.astro";
import PriceCards from "../../components/PriceCards.astro";
import Ask from "../../components/Ask.astro";

import { AREAS } from "../../data/areas";

const area = AREAS.find((a) => a.slug === "areas/kentish-town")!;

const PRICES = [
  {
    art: "fitted-wardrobes",
    name: "Fitted wardrobes",
    href: "/fitted-wardrobes/",
    price: "From £2,300 for a 2-door run up to 1.2m in melamine, and from £4,000 at 4 doors.",
    moves: "Width, finish, internal drawers, integrated lighting and more complex designs move the figure.",
  },
  {
    art: "kitchens",
    name: "Bespoke kitchens",
    href: "/bespoke-kitchens/",
    price: "Typically from £10,000.",
    moves: "Kitchen size and layout, cabinetry and internal storage, worktops, appliances, and materials and finishes move the figure.",
  },
  {
    art: "alcove-units",
    name: "Alcove units",
    href: "/alcove-units/",
    price: "From £3,300 for a pair in melamine, and from £6,000 in oak veneer.",
    moves: "Finish, shelving detail, drawers and lighting move the figure. It assumes base cupboards with shelving over, up to about 1m per alcove.",
  },
];
const P2 = "margin-top:var(--space-10)";
const TEXT = "max-width:var(--measure)";
const SECTION = "padding-bottom:var(--section-y)";
const EXT = { target: "_blank", rel: "noopener" };
---

<AreaPage area={area}>
  <Fragment slot="lede">
    <p>
      We design and install <Link href="/fitted-wardrobes/">fitted wardrobes</Link>, <Link href="/bespoke-kitchens/">bespoke kitchens</Link> and <Link href="/alcove-units/">alcove units</Link> for homes in Kentish Town, on streets like Leighton Road and Kelly Street. In a narrow terraced house, we can take a wardrobe up to the ceiling and plan a kitchen around the route through the room.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Small bedrooms" level={4}>Fitted wardrobes for small bedrooms in Kentish Town</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide below drawing={["kt-wardrobe-top-cupboards", "kt-sliding-doors-plan"]}>
      <p>
        In the narrow houses on Kelly Street and Castlehaven Road, bedrooms can be small. We can run a wardrobe across the full width of the wall, with separate top cupboards for spare bedding and luggage. If there's little room for the doors to swing, we can use <Link href="/journal/built-in-wardrobe-ideas/">sliding doors</Link> instead.
      </p>
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Leighton Road" level={4}>Alcove units for period houses around Leighton Road</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide below drawing="kt-alcoves-measured">
      <p>
        On Leighton Road, Georgian and Victorian houses stand side by side, so next-door houses can have quite different rooms. If you have <Link href="/journal/alcove-units-guide/">alcoves beside a chimney breast</Link>, we'll measure each side on its own, as they aren't always the same size. We can fit wardrobes into bedroom alcoves, or shelves over low cupboards in a living room.
      </p>
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Kitchens" level={4}>Bespoke kitchens for narrow rooms in Kentish Town</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide below drawing={["kt-narrow-kitchen-plan", "kt-appliance-run"]}>
      <p>
        The terraced houses around Lady Margaret Road and Dunolie Road are narrow. If your kitchen is narrow too, we'll plan the cabinets around its doorways, windows and the way you walk through it. We'll also confirm your appliance models and sizes before the final design, so the cabinets around them line up.
      </p>
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Bay windows" level={4}>Window seats for bay windows in Kentish Town</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide below drawing="bay-seat">
      <p>
        Many houses on Falkland Road and Ascham Street have a bay window on the ground floor. If your front room has one, we can fit <Ask q="kentish-town-bay-seat">a window seat that follows the bay's angled sides, with drawers underneath</Ask>. <Link href="/alcove-units/">Alcove units</Link> in the same room can share the seat's finish.
      </p>
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Listed homes" level={4}>Fitted joinery for listed homes in Kentish Town</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide below drawing="kt-skirting-kept">
      <p>
        Some homes on Leighton Road, Leverton Street and Kelly Street are listed, and <Link href="https://www.camden.gov.uk/listed-buildings" {...EXT}>Camden Council's listed buildings page</Link> shows whether yours is one. If your home is listed, the council can tell you whether the joinery you're planning needs listed building consent. Ask before work starts, particularly if original skirting or panelling would come off or change.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        When we survey the room, we'll note its original features and plan the joinery around them.
      </p>
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Access" level={4}>Parking and access on fitting day in Kentish Town</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide below drawing="kt-van-route">
      <p>
        We'll agree with you before fitting day where the van can unload and where it can park. That matters most on a narrow cul-de-sac like Kelly Street, or on the unmade private lane at Torriano Cottages.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        Camden Council's <Link href="https://www.camden.gov.uk/controlled-parking-zones" {...EXT}>parking zones page</Link> has a postcode search, so you can check which zone your street is in. If the van needs a <Link href="https://www.camden.gov.uk/trade-permits" {...EXT}>parking permit</Link>, we'll agree with you which of us arranges it.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        Before anything is carried in on fitting day, we protect the floors and stairs it will pass.
      </p>
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Guide prices" level={4}>What a wardrobe, a kitchen or an alcove pair starts at</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide>
      <p>Every figure here is a starting price, and a larger or more detailed piece costs more.</p>
      <p style={P2}>
        The guarantee covers installation and workmanship for 2 years and the structure of the joinery for 10 years. Hardware is on the manufacturer's warranty, and doors and drawers are adjusted in the first 2 years. Our <Link href="/fitted-wardrobe-cost/">cost guide</Link> shows what moves the price of a fitted wardrobe.
      </p>
      <PriceCards slot="side" items={PRICES} />
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Questions" scribe>Asked before enquiring</SectionHead>
    <div style="margin-top:var(--space-12);max-width:var(--measure);border-bottom:var(--rule-hair)">
      <Faq q="Where should a wardrobe go in a small bedroom?">
        Put it where the route around the bed stays clear and there's room to open its doors and drawers. Decide where the bed and bedside tables go first, then look at the room from the doorway.
      </Faq>
      <Faq q="How do handleless drawers work?">
        A handleless drawer opens with a recessed finger grip or a push-to-open fitting, so nothing sticks out from the front. We'll agree with you how each drawer and door opens before we finish the design.
      </Faq>
      <Faq q="What is a bespoke kitchen?">
        A bespoke kitchen is made to measure and designed around the space in your room. You can choose the door style, such as Shaker or handleless, and the balance of drawers, shelves and cupboards. We plan the storage around what you use every day and where you need it.
      </Faq>
    </div>
  </Wrap>
</AreaPage>
